          }
        },
        {
          "refId": "C",
          "expr": "sum by (store) (rate(sbproxy_embedded_store_operations_total{op=\"evict\"}[5m]))",
          "legendFormat": "evicted {{store}}",
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${DS_PROMETHEUS}"
          }
        }
      ],
      "description": "Two different problems on one axis, kept apart by the outcome label. An error is a fault: the redb transaction failed, or a record could not be decoded, and the subsystem above it did not get the write it asked for. A refusal is the bounded in-memory store working as designed, turning away a write because it is at its entry cap; a sustained refusal rate means a dedup window is too small for the traffic, not that the store is broken. Evictions are the TTL sweep reclaiming expired entries and are healthy at any rate."
    },
